-**Edge Cases:**
-- If $\epsilon$ is too large, significant color transitions may be missed, leading to visual artifacts [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-- If $\epsilon$ is too small, the performance benefit is reduced as more samples are processed [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-- The method assumes that once opacity is very high ($> 1-\epsilon$), further color changes are insignificant [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Precision:** The comparison $\| C_{acc} - C_{prev} \| < \epsilon$ is sensitive to floating-point precision. If $\epsilon$ is too small relative to the precision of $C_{acc}$, the condition may never trigger, negating the benefit.
+*   **Boundary:** The condition $\alpha_{acc} \ge 1 - \epsilon$ ensures that rays passing through fully opaque regions terminate correctly, even if color changes are minimal due to occlusion.
 
